            "short_description": "Abre caminho pelas ruas de Hong Kong num intenso jogo de tiro vertical. Inspirado nos filmes de ação clássicos, The Hong Kong Massacre coloca-te no centro de uma história de vingança fria e crua, repleta de tiroteios cinemáticos brutais e localizações vívidas do submundo.",
            "supported_languages": "Inglês<strong>*</strong><br><strong>*</strong>idiomas com suporte total de áudio",
            "reviews": {
                "query_summary": {
                    "num_reviews": 1,
                    "review_score": 6,
                    "review_score_desc": "Mostly Positive",
                    "total_positive": 843,
                    "total_negative": 341,
                    "total_reviews": 1184
                },
                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "237596221",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198320076431",
                            "num_reviews": 38,
                            "playtime_forever": 198,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 198,
                            "last_played": 1791730712
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I saw this on Steam and thought, \"Top-down shooting, bullet time, and frenetic action? It's like if Max Payne and Hotline Miami had a baby. This must be pure fire.\" Well, half of that thought was right. More specifically, it's like Hotline Miami impregnated Max Payne, who refused to stop smoking, doing drugs, or drinking throughout the pregnancy. The result? A barely functional, heavily deformed baby that even its parents wonder how it's still alive. \n\nThe story is boring, the visuals are bland, and the level design is mediocre. But the gameplay is where things really fall apart. Movement feels clunky and collision detection is a joke. Enemy variety is practically nonexistent, their placement feels random, and the AI is painfully predictable. Then there are the ghost bullets: getting killed by shots that seemingly don't exist, so often that I started recording gameplay to confirm I wasn't losing my mind. The bosses aren't any better. Every fight boils down to running through parallel hallways while shooting, and their movement is tied to yours, making them ridiculously easy to exploit. I breezed through most of the game, only to get absolutely demolished by the first encounter on the final day because apparently that's where the game decided to trap you, because that is the only thing you can describe that level as ... a trap.",
                        "timestamp_created": 1791853622,
                        "timestamp_updated": 1791853622,
                        "voted_up": false,
                        "votes_up": 9,
                        "votes_funny": 6,
                        "weighted_vote_score": 0.5990591049194336,
                        "comment_count": 0,
                        "steam_purchase": true,
                        "received_for_free": false,
                        "written_during_early_access": false,
                        "developer_response": "Hey there,\n\nOh boy, that is one graphic description; gotta admit, we had a laugh. Back to being serious: we're sorry you found the experience so disappointing, and we've taken notes on areas that might need improvement. Do you mind sending us the recordings of said \"ghost bullets\"? I'm curious if it's indeed a bug or a shotgun spread, and if it's a bug it needs, of course, to be fixed. If you could send the video at support@untoldtales.games it'd be very helpful. \n\nKind regards,",
                        "timestamp_dev_responded": 1793619735,
                        "primarily_steam_deck": false,
                        "refunded": false
                    }
                ],
